    "note": "Every story this package ships, and every story it used to ship and deliberately does not. Checked both ways by test/contracts/story-roster.test.ts, which is why adding a story means adding its id here. A story's id is <meta title>::<export name>, the same pair a visual-regression baseline is keyed on, so a retitled meta and a renamed export are caught as well as a deletion. A retired entry is not an apology: it is the record that a capability lost its only demonstration, it reads {\"id\": \"Styles/Label::CornerRadius\", \"why\": \"what was lost and why it could not be migrated\", \"replacedBy\": \"an id that demonstrates the subject now, when one does\"}, and it stays until the story comes back. The twenty stories removed in ff12a515 have all been restored; the history is in design/element-api/capability-losses.md. The roster can only see a story's NAME, so a second gate, test/contracts/story-demonstrates-its-subject.test.ts, reads what each story in the Styles tree actually writes and requires a story named after a style channel to paint that channel -- which is the hole a roster of ids cannot close on its own.",
